WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.214
A hotel books a housekeeper for a
morning shift, and pays up front.

2
00:00:05.614 --> 00:00:07.050
Two things can go wrong.

3
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.119
Nobody accepts the job.

4
00:00:09.319 --> 00:00:11.823
Or someone accepts it, and never shows up.

5
00:00:12.223 --> 00:00:17.081
For the first, every booking has a timer
in the cloud, set for its start time.

6
00:00:17.381 --> 00:00:22.980
If nobody has accepted by then, the booking
cancels itself and the money goes back.

7
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.430
When I came back to it later,

8
00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:29.464
I found an accepted job that
never started just stayed open,

9
00:00:29.464 --> 00:00:31.347
with the hotel's money held.

10
00:00:31.747 --> 00:00:33.454
So I added a no-show rule.

11
00:00:33.754 --> 00:00:38.384
When a provider accepts, a second
timer is set for the end of the shift,

12
00:00:38.384 --> 00:00:39.670
plus a grace period.

13
00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:44.144
If the job never started, the
booking is cancelled and refunded.

14
00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:48.483
Nobody gets a strike for it,
because nobody chose to cancel.

15
00:00:48.983 --> 00:00:49.953
One more catch:

16
00:00:49.953 --> 00:00:54.933
the cloud's task service won't schedule
anything more than thirty days ahead.

17
00:00:55.333 --> 00:01:00.433
So a timer for a booking further out wakes
at twenty-nine days, sees it's early,

18
00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:01.836
and sets itself again.

19
00:01:02.336 --> 00:01:06.237
No booking can get stuck, and
nobody has to watch the clock.
